Hey Philippe,

I disabled ACPI_IOMMU and managed to disable CONFIG_PCI_MSI. However, I
still could not boot into xenomai when the PCI card is plugged it.
I also tried the most recent version of xenomai (2.5.5.1) in combination
with linux 2.6.34.7. But no difference.

I get a 'timer not connected to IO-APIC" error msg (see attached is a
photo).

RIght now I do not have access to the syslog and config files. Will post
them as soon as I get back to work.

Please let me know if there are other things I can try. Unfortunately, I do
not even know the cause of my problem. Is it the way the interrupts are
assigned ? address space collision ?

THANKS, you help is very much appreciated.

 peter


On Sun, Oct 10, 2010 at 2:28 PM, Philippe Gerum <rpm@xenomai.org> wrote:

> On Sun, 2010-10-10 at 12:30 -0700, Peter Pastor wrote:
> > Hey,
> >
> > I have installed xenomai 2.5.5 using linux 2.6.31.8 and the
> > corresponding adeos patch adeos-ipipe-2.6.31.8-x86-2.4-09.patch.
> >
> > I also tried to disable CONFIG_PCI_MSI by passing pci=nomsi in grub as
> > documented in /usr/src/linux/Documentation/kernel-parameters.txt. I
> > did not disable the CONFIG_PCI_MSI option since it is selected by:
> > AMD_IOMMU && X86_64 && PCI && ACPI ...and I did not know which of
> > these options I should deselect. Also, when I disable ACPI completely,
> > the kernel only detects a single cpu, although there are 8 cores in
> > the system.
>
> Disabling ACPI completely is a dead end; however you may try to disable
> AMD_IOMMU if you don't have this feature supported in your chipset.
>
> --
> Philippe.
